<!-- generated by driftgate; edit .driftgate/ instead -->

- `pnpm install` · `pnpm build` · `pnpm test` (Vitest) · `pnpm lint` · `pnpm verify`
- `pnpm -r exec tsc --noEmit` — typecheck across the workspace
- Single test file: `pnpm vitest run <path>` · single case: add `-t "<name>"`
- `DRIFTGATE_TEST_DIST=1 pnpm test` — exercises the **built** binary. The normal suite
  aliases `@driftgate/*` to source, so nothing else catches a broken `exports` map or a
  regressed exit code. Run it after `pnpm build`; it has already caught one real
  regression.

Golden fixtures live in `fixtures/<tool>/{input,expected}` and are asserted byte-exact;
detect fixtures use `fixtures/<tool>-detect/{positive,negative}`, and import fixtures use
`fixtures/<tool>-import/{input,expected}` — a repo with the tool's native files and no
`.driftgate/`, plus the canonical rules `read()` must recover from it. Regenerate with
`pnpm build && pnpm fixtures:update` — it prints the change set and writes nothing without
`--yes`, and it refuses to run when `CI` is set. See `fixtures/README.md`.

`driftgate init` is the on-ramp for a repository that has never used Driftgate: it
detects which tools are configured, imports their configs into canonical rules, collapses
the copies that arrive from several tools at once, and prints every file it would create,
modify or leave alone. It **writes nothing without `--yes`**. Applying takes ownership of
the files it imported from, copying each original to `.driftgate/backup/` first. On a
repository that already has a `.driftgate/` it says so and does nothing.

`driftgate sync` also **removes the artifacts no enabled adapter produces any more** —
delete a rule and its `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` goes with it, instead of being left on disk at
exit 0 for Cursor to keep loading. Every removal is copied to `.driftgate/backup/` first,
and an orphan edited since Driftgate wrote it is refused rather than deleted. Set
`options.backup: false` in the manifest to delete without the copy.

`driftgate check` is `sync`'s read-only twin: it renders the same plan in memory, compares
it to disk, and exits 1 with a unified diff when they differ. **Clean means `sync` would
write nothing and delete nothing** — a stale artifact, a hand-edited one, a missing one, a
file Driftgate never generated standing where its output goes, and an orphan still on disk
all count, each with the recovery hint `sync` would give. The diff runs from disk to
render, so `+` lines are what `sync` will write; changed lines show trailing spaces and
tabs as `·` and `␉`, and a missing final newline is marked, because those are the
differences a hash sees and a diff otherwise hides. It holds a filesystem with no write
methods on it and never calls `applyPlan`. `--staged` is not implemented yet: reading the
git index means spawning `git`, and that arrives with the pre-commit hook package (T052).

`driftgate restore [path...]` puts back the originals under `.driftgate/backup/` — the undo
for both `sync --force` and orphan deletion. Like `init` it **writes nothing without
`--yes`**, and it copies raw bytes, so a CRLF or BOM original comes back exactly as it was.
With no arguments it restores everything in the backup.

`driftgate doctor` reports which tools are configured, which files each will actually
load, in what order, whether they are in sync, and roughly what they cost in tokens. It is
read-only and **exits 0 even when it has warnings** — `check` owns exit 1 for drift, and a
command that reports a correct permanent condition as a CI failure is one people mute.
`--no-global` skips the user-level probe; `--json` emits the whole report.

Exit codes: `0` ok · `1` drift or failure · `2` usage. CI reads the code, not the
message, so a usage error must never be reported as drift.
